 ## Overview
-Seed Line Integral Convolution (Seed LIC) is a volume rendering technique for visualizing 3D vector fields that computes Line Integral Convolution (LIC) only along field lines starting from specific seed points, rather than filling the entire volume [S1, S2]. It solves the computational bottleneck of standard Fast LIC [7940-line-integral-convolution-lic.md] by exploiting the sparsity of the input texture, calculating field lines and convolution only from distributed seed points instead of every voxel [S1, S2]. Unlike Fast LIC, which assigns values to neighboring voxels causing smearing, Seed LIC only assigns values to voxels along field lines from seed points, resulting in sparser textures but significantly reduced computational cost [S1, S2].
+Seed Line Integral Convolution (Seed LIC) is a volume rendering method for visualizing 3D vector fields that computes Line Integral Convolution (LIC) only along field lines originating from a sparse set of seed points, rather than covering every voxel in the output texture [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of high computational cost associated with Fast LIC when applied to large 3D datasets by exploiting the sparsity of the input texture [S1, S2]. Unlike its sibling [Image Space Fabric Visualization](10569-image-space-fabric-visualization.md) which renders explicit geometric primitives, Seed LIC generates a sparse texture of field lines, setting it apart from [Line Integral Convolution (LIC)](7940-line-integral-convolution-lic.md) by avoiding unnecessary computations in areas where field lines do not coincide with cells containing seed points [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Input:** A 3D vector field $\mathbf{v}$, a scalar field defining a Region of Interest (ROI) or random distribution criteria, and parameters for seed point generation (density factor, threshold, minimum distance) [S1, S2].
-- **Output:** A 3D scalar texture representing the LIC result, where voxel values indicate the integrated noise along streamlines [S1, S2].
+*   **Input:** A 3D vector field $\mathbf{V}$, a scalar field for Region Of Interest (ROI) definition (e.g., vorticity magnitude), and a density factor $\delta \in [0,1]$ controlling seed point sparsity [S1, S2].
+*   **Output:** A 3D scalar texture where voxel values represent the convolved intensity of field lines, normalized to the range $[0, 255]$ [S1, S2].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- **Input Texture ($T$):** A 3D grid of voxels. Initially, all voxels are set to 0. Voxels containing seed points are set to 255 (or a non-zero value) [S1, S2].
-- **Output Texture ($I$):** A 3D grid of voxels, initialized to 0 [S1, S2].
-- **Field Line State:** Current position $\mathbf{x}$, integration step $m$, and accumulated convolution value [G].
-- **Minimum Distance:** The minimum distance between seed points to prevent merging [S1, S2].
-- **Filter Kernel Length ($2L$):** The length of the convolution kernel [S1].
-- **Field Line Length:** The total length of the field line integration [S1].
-- **Integration Limit ($M$):** The maximum number of steps for the while loop in the convolution [S2].
+*   **Input Texture ($T$):** A 3D grid of the same resolution as the vector field (or an enlarged resolution for detail enlargement). Voxels are initialized to 0. Selected seed voxels are set to 255 [S1, S2].
+*   **Output Texture ($I$):** A 3D grid of the same resolution as the input texture. Initialized to 0. Stores the convolution result [S1, S2].
+*   **Hit Count Map ($H$):** A 3D grid storing the number of field lines passing through each voxel, used for normalization [S2].
 
 **Parameters:**
-- **Density Factor ($d$):** A value in $[0, 1]$ controlling the sparsity of the input texture [S1, S2].
-- **Threshold Value:** A scalar value threshold used to determine if a voxel contributes to the input texture based on the ROI [S2].
-- **Minimum Distance:** Sources specify discarding points within three voxels of previously inserted seeds [S1, S2].
-- **Filter Kernel Length ($2L$):** In reported experiments, set to 21 times the grid cell size [S1].
-- **Field Line Length:** In reported experiments, set to 51 times the size of the grid cells [S1].
-- **Integration Limit ($M$):** Derived from field line length; typically $M = \text{line\_length\_cells} // 2$ [S2].
+*   **Filter Kernel Length ($2L$):** Typically set to 21 times the grid cell size [S1].
+*   **Field Line Length:** Typically set to 51 times the grid cell size [S1].
+*   **Minimum Seed Distance:** Points within 3 voxels of an existing seed are discarded to prevent merging [S1, S2].
+*   **Threshold Value:** Scalar value threshold for ROI inclusion [S2].
+*   **Density Factor ($\delta$):** Probability threshold for seed placement [S2].
 
